<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:34:13Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-ger" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-1432?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Merz says next German president 'must be a woman' — report</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:22071af762fadbd96dcb7bc2fcb03de1af2167c860c20c9d91b7b6051e30c720</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:34:13Z</updated><published>2026-11-02T07:36:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/merz-says-next-german-president-must-be-a-woman-report/live-79825749--f592c9770ada.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/merz-says-next-german-president-must-be-a-woman-report/live-79825749?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German President Frank-Walter Steinmeier's second term is due to end in March next year. Meanwhile, Merz has called for 'digital sovereignty' in the era of AI. Follow DW for more</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>German police investigating after needles found in Halloween candy</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:75b1315dea8ef0e8de0a29c7b3404428c5d627c1fa93bd29f0f78fc5244f663d</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-11-02T01:16:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/german-police-investigating-after-needles-found-in-halloween-candy/a-79825051--00c6413d6c8f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/german-police-investigating-after-needles-found-in-halloween-candy/a-79825051?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German police said they received phone calls with reports about sharp objects concealed in Halloween candy.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Gen Z group calls for new protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:85a80d9861e8f3a4b9516deda8c4f3862705499209a10157c6a08d63c7416d22</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-11-01T12:26:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-gen-z-group-calls-for-new-protests/live-79814961--f0055b176099.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-gen-z-group-calls-for-new-protests/live-79814961?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The group behind German demonstrations this weekend has called for more protests to keep pressure on the government to act. Meanwhile, the IG Metall union has begun warning strikes after pay talks collapse. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany's Gen Z protests disrupted by violence on Saturday</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3e449867d077420ad767df2a9811d17375367ce712b456925605c5187be90dab</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-31T16:00:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-gen-z-protests-disrupted-by-violence-on-saturday/live-79814694--76fbedb708ba.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-gen-z-protests-disrupted-by-violence-on-saturday/live-79814694?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Nationwide protests led by young people were largely peaceful throughout the day, but reports about looting and rioting in the evening, especially in Frankfurt, overshadowed the spirit of the demonstrations.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany's search for a nuclear waste site</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fe0426eb0e049229f426079da24bcab29e0a873c213cee07f2ea526897d3589f</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-11-01T08:06:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-search-for-a-nuclear-waste-site/a-79820678--66cee2f12295.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-search-for-a-nuclear-waste-site/a-79820678?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Finland has become the first country with a final storage facility for nuclear waste. Germany is still searching for a site, while some countries have yet to start. Costs are expected to total many billions of euros.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>Germany takes cautious approach on Israeli election results</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dbb79ea56d16f6da20cae9ce9eaad49f4b9bb5a006689859361aa864b8b4aa1a</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T11:26:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-takes-cautious-approach-on-israeli-election-results/a-79805184--6eb977f23eb2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-takes-cautious-approach-on-israeli-election-results/a-79805184?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German politicians have been slow to respond as they wait to see who is likely to comprise the next coalition. Many are also hoping for a government without religious hardliners.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>The dachshund, a humorous icon with political symbolism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcad094f4a9f7f57f486e175adf4a7fddc27e16b2628019f32c6322923422652</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T07:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191--0ec0144d606e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Picasso to Andy Warhol, a surprising number of artists have portrayed the German breed. But the dachshund has also at times been co-opted as a political symbol.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Wagner brothers begin new NBA chapter on different paths</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c1c4a17104858ee9be801111eec1c5da027abd9daf4fc2dfb72f9d6edb16e75a</id><updated>2026-11-02T18:32:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T12:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/wagner-brothers-begin-new-nba-chapter-on-different-paths/a-79712668--ff5544415c4f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/wagner-brothers-begin-new-nba-chapter-on-different-paths/a-79712668?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After five years, brothers Franz and Moritz Wagner are no longer playing together for the Orlando Magic. In the new NBA season, their separation will likely mean the new "German" team is the Charlotte Hornets.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry></feed>
